    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm a bit confused by your last point. Could you stick to the actual chemical mechanism? I'd like to focus on the specific role of the enzyme alliinase in converting the amino acid sulfoxides into sulfenic acids, and then how that leads to the formation of the volatile gas propanethial S-oxide. Please avoid the metaphorical language and just give me the step-by-step molecular pathway.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's delve into the molecular pathway without the goblin metaphors. When you cut an onion, the cells are damaged, and the enzyme alliinase is released. Alliinase is a cysteine protease that specifically targets the amino acid sulfoxides present in the onion cells. The primary sulfoxide involved is S-1-propenyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide (PCS).\n\nUpon exposure to the enzyme, alliinase catalyzes the conversion of PCS to S-1-propenyl-L-cysteine sulfenic acid (PCS-SOH). This reaction is the first step in the formation of the volatile compound responsible for the eye irritation. PCS-SOH is unstable and quickly rearranges into S-1-propenyl-L-cysteine sulfenamide (PCS-SNAc).\n\nThe next step involves the oxidation of PCS-SNAc by molecular oxygen (O2) to form S-1-propenyl-L-cysteine sulfenic acid (PCS-SOH) again, but this time it is a different sulfenic acid. This sulfenic acid then reacts with another PCS molecule to form S-1-propenyl-L-cysteine sulfenamide (PCS-SNAc",
      "source": "target",
